Now I have the full picture. This is an MSTest 4.x integration test class targeting .NET 10, with the `[TestCategory("Integration")]` marker. Let me deliver the formal audit.

---

## Test Smell Audit — `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`

**Framework:** MSTest 4.1.0 · .NET 10 · Integration scope (`[TestCategory("Integration")]`)
**Catalog:** testsmells.org 19-smell academic taxonomy (Peruma et al., ESEC/FSE 2020)
**Calibration:** Integration-test rules applied — Mystery Guest, Eager Test, and Sleepy Test severity adjusted for integration scope where appropriate.

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 1              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

8 test methods scanned · 4 tests clean · **50% smell-free rate**

---

### Findings by Severity

#### ❶ Sleepy Test — HIGH

**Affected:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);  // ← blocks for 3 seconds, non-deterministic
```

Even in integration tests, `Thread.Sleep` is a smell (Camara et al., SAST 2021 correlate it directly with flakiness). The 3-second wait is arbitrary — too short on a slow CI agent, needlessly slow locally.

**Fix — poll with a timeout:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    // Poll up to 5 s, fail fast when ready
    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow.AddSeconds(5);
    while (!_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
    {
        Assert.IsTrue(DateTime.UtcNow < deadline, "Notification was not sent within 5 s.");
        await Task.Delay(100);
    }
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky CI failures; suite slowed by hard-coded delays that only grow over time.

---

#### ❷ Conditional Test Logic — HIGH

**Affected:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101-108)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

The `if`/`else` means only one branch ever executes per run. If the production code returns `PremiumUser` today, the `else` branch is dead code — or vice versa. Hidden test gaps.

**Fix — split into two explicit tests:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasPositiveDiscountRate()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com") as PremiumUser;

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** One logical path is never tested — bugs hide behind the branch that always wins.

---

#### ❸ Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test) — HIGH

**Affected:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113-119)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← no assertion anywhere
}
```

The name implies "does not throw," but the test framework reports `Passed` even if every insert silently corrupts data. This gives false confidence.

**Fix — assert the observable outcome:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** 100% false-pass rate — any silent corruption, constraint violation, or no-op implementation goes undetected.

---

#### ❹ Assertion Roulette — MEDIUM

**Affected:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35-38)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);           // which one failed?
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

Four assertions, zero message parameters. When one fails in CI, the stack trace points to a line number but gives no semantic context — you must open the file to understand the failure.

**Fix — add descriptive messages:**
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip through persistence");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip through persistence");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Persisted user should have a positive database-assigned Id");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower triage — developers must reproduce locally to understand CI failures instead of reading the message.

---

### Smell-Free Patterns ✅

These 4 tests are well-structured and serve as good examples for the team:

| Test | Why it's clean |
|------|---------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange-act-assert; 2 focused assertions |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single assertion on the exact expected outcome |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count assertion is contextually self-documenting (3 inserts → 3 count); `CollectionAssert` is idiomatic |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Proper async/await; count matches Range(0,10); tests a meaningful concurrency scenario |

The in-memory SQLite pattern (`Data Source=:memory:`) is also excellent — it avoids the **Mystery Guest** smell entirely by keeping the database hermetic and disposable.

---

### Smells Not Detected (15 of 19 clean)

| Smell | Status |
|-------|--------|
| Mystery Guest | ✅ Clean — in-memory SQLite is hermetic |
| Eager Test | ✅ Clean — no test calls 4+ production methods (downgraded for integration scope as well) |
| Magic Number Test | ✅ Clean — all numeric literals are contextually self-documenting |
| Sensitive Equality | ✅ Clean — no `ToString()` comparisons |
| Exception Handling | ✅ Clean — no `try`/`catch` in tests |
| General Fixture | ✅ Clean — both fields used by all 8 tests |
| Ignored/Disabled Test | ✅ Clean — no `[Ignore]` annotations |
| Duplicate Assert | ✅ Clean |
| Lazy Test | ✅ Clean |
| Constructor Initialization | ✅ Clean — uses `[TestInitialize]` correctly |
| Default Test | ✅ Clean — proper class naming |
| Redundant Print | ✅ Clean |
| Redundant Assertion | ✅ Clean |
| Resource Optimism | ✅ Clean |
| Empty Test | ✅ Clean |

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Impact | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|--------|
| **1** | Assertion-Free Test | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, ...)` after bulk insert | Eliminates a 100% false-pass test | ~2 min |
| **2** | Conditional Test Logic | Split into 2 tests with explicit setup per type | Closes hidden test gap | ~10 min |
| **3** | Sleepy Test | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with poll loop + deadline | Fixes flakiness + speeds up suite | ~10 min |
| **4** | Assertion Roulette | Add message strings to 4 assertions | Faster failure triage | ~5 min |

**Bottom line:** The suite is in reasonable shape — 50% of tests are clean, and the fixture and data patterns are solid. The 3 high-severity findings are straightforward to fix and would meaningfully improve reliability and diagnostic speed.
